{
  "id": "80523",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Dynamic Equilibrium ",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Equilibrium"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A dynamic process in which the concentrations of reactants and products remain constant and the forward and reverse reactions occur at equal rates."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1499866623",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2102054063",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " is a state in which opposing processes occur at the same rate such that the amounts of substances within a closed system remain constant over time. Equilibrium can be established within a closed system for physical processes such as condensation and evaporation and chemical reactions. This unit focuses on equilibria of chemical processes (chemical equilibrium), starting with a look at how equilibrium is established in a system with opposing physical processes. We use water evaporation to illustrate several important aspects of equilibrium."
              }
            ],
            "id": "9e4yi91e5w8ft0p",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "When liquid water is poured into an empty bottle at room temperature, some water evaporates, the amount of liquid decreases, and the amount of water vapor increases. If a cap is placed on the bottle so no water vapor escapes, the amount of liquid H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O and gaseous H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O will eventually stop changing, and an equilibrium between the liquid and the vapor will be established.  If the bottle is not capped, the water vapor will escape until all of the liquid water has been converted into water vapor, and equilibrium will not be reached. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "5676oydqymse4u0",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Equilibrium cannot be achieved in an open system that allows the vapor to escape."
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3175498843",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "sm6osy7mmr9a3w4",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "dymrh3gcuqrht96",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/ac/acfdb74077ed04026e506bccd716e556/open_close_equilibrium.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A sealed bottle containing H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "l"
              },
              {
                "text": ") and H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "O("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "g"
              },
              {
                "text": ") is an example of a closed system. Equilibrium can be achieved only if the system is closed.  When the cap is put on the bottle, the liquid water being converted to water vapor is trapped in the system. Subsequently, some of the water vapor that forms will be converted back to liquid water. These two opposing processes, "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": true,
                "src": "{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)\\;\\;\\rightarrow\\;\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(g)\\\\\\\\{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(g)\\;\\;\\rightarrow\\;\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": "occur simultaneously. The rate of the liquid being converted to vapor and the rate of the vapor being converted to liquid eventually will be equal. At that point, the amount of water vapor and liquid water will remain constant over time. In the figure below, equilibrium is established at time "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "t"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "5"
              },
              {
                "text": ". We know that equilibrium is established because the amounts of water vapor particles and liquid water particles remain constant over time. The equilibrium that is established will remain constant indefinitely unless a change is made to the system."
              }
            ],
            "id": "g920lom2i9kz7ih",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Equilibrium is reached when, over time, the amounts of water vapor and liquid water remain constant, as seen at "
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "t"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "5"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", t"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "and "
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "t"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "7"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "234270387",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ya6gytnpgecpgkj",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "lmvh27bfvokvuh0",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/c4/c4251acb1b361151a6434cf59bc28a43/new_equilibrium_est.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "One such change we can make to the system that will alter the equilibrium is to increase the temperature. If we heat the capped bottle to a new temperature, more of the liquid water will be converted to water vapor. At the higher temperature, a new equilibrium will be established with different proportions of liquid water and water vapor compared to the previous equilibrium at the lower temperature. This new equilibrium will remain constant indefinitely over time unless another change is made to the system. In the figure below, we can see that equilibrium is established at "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "t"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "4"
              },
              {
                "text": ", because it is at this time that the proportion of liquid water to water vapor remains constant over time."
              }
            ],
            "id": "oc6h3pojlz4cj8v",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "At "
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "t"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "4"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": ", equilibrium is reached, and the proportion of liquid to vapor remains constant at "
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "t"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "5"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " and "
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "t"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "928525733",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "56jqnq1inrgfych",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "papa8eot91dy0uq",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/53/539192dd11cdcc90691a0d13e55d9821/heated_equilibrium.png",
            "type": "img"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "It is important to emphasize that both physical, as described above, and chemical, involving a chemical reaction, equilibria are dynamic. A reaction or physical process at equilibrium does not \"stop” but instead proceeds in opposing directions, forward and reverse, at the same rate. This dynamic nature is essential to understanding equilibrium behavior. The animation below illustrates the dynamic nature of equilibrium at the molecular level and the difference between open and closed systems.  "
              }
            ],
            "id": "3136914549",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "oc5pxldcxc2xj0o",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "9tts6pxdbqnyeyu",
            "src": "JsoawKguU6A",
            "type": "youtube"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "  "
              }
            ],
            "id": "hm2iq49x600o3j7",
            "type": "p"
          }
